1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,490
You recreate movement and jumping from a game
you love,

2
00:00:03,490 --> 00:00:07,132
but playing your version feels unexpectedly
different.

3
00:00:07,132 --> 00:00:09,877
You may have transferred visible features

4
00:00:09,877 --> 00:00:13,002
while missing what players noticed and
decided.

5
00:00:13,002 --> 00:00:16,580
Jumps in Super Meat Boy, dashes in Hollow
Knight,

6
00:00:16,580 --> 00:00:21,292
and movement through portals all involve
different decisions.

7
00:00:21,292 --> 00:00:29,680
Let us observe small actions, change their
conditions, and build rules for our own game.

8
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:34,150
First, break an enjoyable moment into a small
action.

9
00:00:34,150 --> 00:00:37,982
Go beyond saying that it looks beautiful or
feels good.

10
00:00:37,982 --> 00:00:41,592
Before a jump, you read the platform and gap.
In the air,

11
00:00:41,592 --> 00:00:45,672
you adjust your landing. After landing, you
check the result.

12
00:00:45,672 --> 00:00:48,001
Write down noticing, choosing, acting,

13
00:00:48,001 --> 00:00:52,042
and recognizing the outcome. This gives your
analysis a clear target.

14
00:00:52,042 --> 00:00:58,607
In Hollow Knight, examine when and where a
dash is used, and where it leaves the player.

15
00:00:58,607 --> 00:01:03,440
Copying a character color does not reproduce
those decisions.

16
00:01:04,160 --> 00:01:07,744
Second, turn a feeling into conditions you can
examine.

17
00:01:07,744 --> 00:01:11,862
Saying that a dash feels exciting does not
specify what to build.

18
00:01:11,862 --> 00:01:17,019
Is it the speed, passing through danger, or
being able to try again quickly?

19
00:01:17,019 --> 00:01:20,702
The same action may be enjoyable for several
reasons.

20
00:01:20,702 --> 00:01:24,239
Portal is more than an effect showing someone
entering a doorway.

21
00:01:24,239 --> 00:01:28,482
You consider where entrances and exits should
go to move yourself and objects.

22
00:01:28,482 --> 00:01:31,978
These examples are comparisons for describing
decisions, risks,

23
00:01:31,978 --> 00:01:36,160
and outcomes more precisely. They are not
ready-made answers for your own game.

24
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:41,703
Now change one condition at a time in an
original prototype. Keep the jump and

25
00:01:41,703 --> 00:01:46,902
platforms alike, but make the landing zone
wide on one side and narrow on the other.

26
00:01:46,902 --> 00:01:51,831
Both versions use a jump button, but the
narrow landing asks for more attention to

27
00:01:51,831 --> 00:01:56,832
timing and adjustment. Keeping other
conditions alike makes comparison easier.

28
00:01:56,832 --> 00:02:01,743
Next, compare immediate retries with a brief
wait after failure.

29
00:02:01,743 --> 00:02:06,742
Does the change help players learn the rule,
or simply add waiting?

30
00:02:06,742 --> 00:02:13,650
Changing speed, artwork, and rewards together
makes their effects hard to separate.

31
00:02:13,650 --> 00:02:17,680
A small test should first help you examine an
idea.

32
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:24,063
Third, choose a decision you want to preserve
and combine it with different rules.

33
00:02:24,063 --> 00:02:28,822
Imagine enjoying the decision to wait briefly
before crossing a gap.

34
00:02:28,822 --> 00:02:34,340
That decision could also appear in a puzzle
about passing through a moving

35
00:02:34,340 --> 00:02:39,502
doorway or a stealth activity that permits
movement during a signal.

36
00:02:39,502 --> 00:02:44,650
Our three prototypes reuse simple shapes for
jumping, crossing, and moving.

37
00:02:44,650 --> 00:02:49,322
Different signals and restart positions
produce different choices.

38
00:02:49,322 --> 00:02:52,238
These prototypes are not proven to be fun.

39
00:02:52,238 --> 00:02:58,320
Ask playtesters what they watched and when
they intended to act, then revise the rules.

40
00:02:59,040 --> 00:03:04,458
When analyzing a game, record one play
sequence rather than only a feature list.

41
00:03:04,458 --> 00:03:08,582
What did you notice, choose, and use to
recognize the result?

42
00:03:08,582 --> 00:03:13,746
Change one condition, then put the decision
you want to keep into a new situation.

43
00:03:13,746 --> 00:03:17,542
Check whether your explanation holds up in a
playable test.

44
00:03:17,542 --> 00:03:21,593
Understanding the experience behind a finished
design

45
00:03:21,593 --> 00:03:25,902
can help you more than transferring its
finished solution.

46
00:03:25,902 --> 00:03:28,439
Choose one recent moment you enjoyed.

47
00:03:28,439 --> 00:03:32,560
Start with the decision you made, and turn it
into a small test.
